Here's one way to solve this problem using the sliding window technique in JavaScript:

```javascript
let lengthOfLongestSubstring = function(s) {

    // define variables
    let maxSubstringLength = 0;
    let currentSubstringLength = 0;
    let substringIndex = new Map();

    for (let i = 0, j = 0; j < s.length; j++) {
        // check if current character already exists in substring
        if (substringIndex.has(s[j])) {
            // update i to skip the repeating characters
            i = Math.max(substringIndex.get(s[j]), i);
        }

        // update variables
        currentSubstringLength = j - i + 1;
        maxSubstringLength = Math.max(maxSubstringLength, currentSubstringLength);
        substringIndex.set(s[j], j + 1);
    }

    return maxSubstringLength;
};

// example
let input = "abcabcbb";
let result = lengthOfLongestSubstring(input);
console.log(result); // Output: 3
```

The code maintains a sliding window by keeping track of the start (`i`) and end (`j`) of the current substring. We also use a map to store the index of the last occurrence of the current character. If the current character already exists in the substring, we update `i` to skip the repeating characters. Then, we update the length of the current substring and the max substring length, and finally update the map with the current character index.

